      <section>
        <h2>Three ways to power the boom: America, China, Europe</h2>
        <p class="answer">The same AI compute demand hits three power systems and
          produces three completely different answers. America builds where the
          market lets it and routes around a slow grid. China decides by policy
          where compute goes and moves the power to meet it. Europe caps what the
          grid will bear and forces efficiency and heat reuse. The constraint is
          identical, gigawatts faster than wires; the response is a fingerprint of
          each system.</p>

        <div class="dom">
          <h3>America: the market races the grid</h3>
          <p>US siting is developer-led, and the binding constraint is time to
            power. Interconnection queues in the big grid regions run for years, so
            hyperscalers route around them: dedicated gas microgrids, grid-scale
            batteries, and direct deals with nuclear plants, including the small
            modular reactor off-takes tracked above. The grid is the bottleneck and
            private capital is the workaround, which is exactly why the
            behind-the-meter and nuclear deals in this section exist.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="dom">
          <h3>China: the state moves the power to the compute</h3>
          <p>China's answer is spatial and directed from the center. The East Data
            West Computing program (东数西算), launched by the National Development
            and Reform Commission in 2022, routes latency-tolerant AI training and
            storage to eight hub clusters in resource-rich western provinces, Inner
            Mongolia, Guizhou, Gansu, and Ningxia, near the solar and wind
            megacomplexes, and carries the electricity east on ultra-high-voltage
            lines. Efficiency is mandated, not encouraged: national hub data centers
            must hit a power usage effectiveness of 1.25 or better. Where America
            waits for a queue, China approves the site and the transmission together.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="dom">
          <h3>Europe: the grid sets the ceiling</h3>
          <p>Europe's constraint is regulatory and local, and its response is
            efficiency plus circularity rather than raw scale. Grid operators in the
            established hubs, Dublin, Frankfurt, Amsterdam, have imposed connection
            caps or moratoriums, pushing new AI builds to the Nordics for hydro and
            free cooling and to southern Europe for cheap solar. Under the EU Energy
            Efficiency Directive, facilities above 500 kW must report power and water
            figures, and waste heat is increasingly piped into municipal district
            heating. Ireland is the sharpest case: its Central Statistics Office puts
            data centers at 23 percent of national metered electricity in 2025, up
            from 5 percent in 2015, and new connections now require meeting at least
            80 percent of annual demand from new renewables.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="meta-line">Ireland figure: <a
          href="https://www.cso.ie/en/statistics/energy/datacentresmeteredelectricityconsumption/"
          rel="noopener">Ireland CSO</a> · China program and efficiency targets:
          NDRC East Data West Computing · Europe reporting: EU Energy Efficiency
          Directive. National electricity-share and total-consumption figures
          circulating for these three regions come from commercial analyst models
          without a citable primary source, so this encyclopedia states the
          paradigm and the sourced anchors, not the disputed totals.</p>
      </section>

